      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When iron or manganese becomes deficient due to high $\\text{pH}$, the vine's roots cannot efficiently absorb these essential micronutrients, leading to physiological stress. Visually, iron deficiency often manifests as chlorosis—yellowing between the leaf veins—especially in younger leaves, while manganese deficiency can cause interveinal chlorosis and sometimes necrotic spots. These symptoms reduce the vine's photosynthetic capability, ultimately impacting berry quality and yield.",
      "source": "target",
