      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "As soil $\\text{pH}$ increases, the concentration of hydroxide ions ($\\text{OH}^-$) in the soil solution rises, which drives the precipitation of metal hydroxides. For example, transition metals like $\\text{Fe}^{2+}$ and $\\text{Mn}^{2+}$ react with these excess $\\text{OH}^-$ ions to form insoluble metal hydroxides, such as $\\text{Fe}(\\text{OH})_3$. This process effectively sequesters the metal ions into solid, unavailable forms within the soil matrix, drastically lowering their concentration in the pore water required for root uptake.",
